roller","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foam_roller"},{"title":"Delayed onset muscle soreness","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delayed_onset_muscle_soreness"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Physique","chain":["Less muscle soreness","Quicker recovery between sessions","More training, more muscle"],"effect":"indirect","text":"Foam rollers and massage tools ease soreness and loosen tight muscles so you can train more often and move better, supporting physique and posture. Studies report short-term gains in range of motion; appearance has not been studied."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":null,"lead":["Foam rollers are lightweight cylinders of elastomeric foam that a person places between a muscle and the floor and rolls over using body weight, a practice classed as self-myofascial release.[1] Proponents use them, along with related massage tools, to increase flexibility, reduce soreness and ease muscle knots.[1]","In posture and body-recovery discussions, such tools are grouped as recovery gear. Published research summarized on Wikipedia reports a short-term improvement in joint range of motion, unknown long-term effects, and a view that the evidence better supports foam rolling as a warm-up activity than as a recovery tool, although post-exercise rolling has been reported to reduce perceived muscle pain.[1]"],"infobox":[["Type","Self-massage / recovery tool"],["Form","Foam cylinders of varying firmness"],["Claimed uses","Flexibility, soreness relief, muscle knots"],["Regulatory status","General consumer product"]],"sections":[{"h":"Description","paras":["A foam roller is used by lying or leaning on it so that body weight presses a muscle against the cylinder, which is then rolled back and forth. Firmness ranges from soft to firm, and softer versions are generally suggested for beginners.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Effectiveness","paras":["A 2015 review by Cheatham and colleagues found a short-term positive effect on joint range of motion, while a 2019 review by Hughes and Ramer concluded that long-term benefits are unknown.[1] A 2021 analysis by Konrad and colleagues found no significant range-of-motion difference between combining rolling with stretching and doing either alone.[1]","A meta-analysis by Wiewelhove and colleagues concluded that the evidence appears to justify foam rolling mainly as a warm-up activity rather than a recovery tool, while also reporting reduced muscle pain perception after post-exercise rolling.[1] Hughes and Ramer suggested that roughly 90 seconds per muscle group may be the minimum for a short-term reduction in soreness, but noted insufficient evidence on the optimal amount.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Relation to muscle soreness","paras":["Delayed onset muscle soreness typically peaks 24 to 72 hours after unaccustomed exercise and usually resolves within about a week.[2] Measures that increase blood flow, including massage, are described as possibly helping somewhat, but the article does not provide detailed evidence on massage.[2]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["Human trials of foam rolling exist for range of motion, warm-up and soreness outcomes, as summarized above.[1] No studies found specific to appearance or posture outcomes in looksmaxxing contexts."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[],"refs":[{"title":"Foam roller","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foam_roller","source":"Wikipedia","year":2025,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Delayed onset muscle soreness","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delayed_onset_muscle_soreness","source":"Wikipedia","year":2025,"type":"encyclopedia"}],"studies":[{"title":"Foam roller","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foam_roller","source":"Wikipedia","year":2025,"kind":null}],"reddit":[],"forums":[{"title":"Is bonesmashing + massage gun cope?","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/is-bonesmashing-massage-gun-cope.1791337/","site":"looksmax.org","archived":"https://web.archive.org/web/20251229070106/https://looksmax.org/threads/is-bonesmashing-massage-gun